A||||7||Tryxalon||High idle and electrical system? ||||5||
Z||000000||Tryxalon||03-17-2001||08:05 PM||||My 85 SE 2.5L 5sp has been running at a very high idle (1200 to 2000) RPM. True I'm only driving it about 18 miles twice a day, but it "isn't right". <P>From comments and troubleshooting I had determined that the Idle was caused by either the EGR or the TPS malfunctioning.  Intentions have been to correct this with the warmer weather, but before that could happen, the starter died. It had been slowly doing so for the past three months ... and finally gave up. <P>After pulling the starter and figuring out what happened (inquiring mind) I noted that the selenoid was "cooked" by the nearby exhaust pipes ... so brittle that the 14+V direct line from the battery  and the "S" connections had both crystalized and the surrounding bakelite had carbonized to the point that resistance was some 800 ohms ALONG the connection path. <P>Replaced the starter, the car started up immediately, cleaned up and took a little drive. <P>Idle has returned to 800 rpm. <P>The only logical explaination I can think of is that the increased resistance drained the battery every time I started the car and the ECM "upped" the idle to charge the car. Since I wasn't driving far or long enough to FULLY charge the battery before I started it  and again drained the battery. <P>Comments?  Am I totally "lost in space" ???   ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||03-17-2001||08:31 PM||||I don't know where in space you may be, but you appear to have found what was the cause of your problems.  A good bit of information.  A "simple" fix for a big problem.  You probably saved yourself $$$ by finding and replacing the "baked" parts.<P>Electrical problems are a pain.||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||03-17-2001||08:44 PM||||I have the same problem with the same car, I replaced the trottle cable (pain in the @$$) I replaced the IAC Valve and lots of other little things i tryed (plugs, oil change, filters...)...  what was the problem though? it sounded to me like you said the starter, that makes no sence?<P>Well i also have another problem right now as of last night, my clutch dont work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red)<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>     <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||03-17-2001||09:06 PM||||Hmmmm....Im having the same prob..only this is on an 87 GT V6......<BR>Is it possible for the same prob on a V6?||||reg||1
Z||000004||KRMFiero||03-17-2001||09:09 PM||||I was just thinking, i dont drive it more then 5 miles a day at the max, i have it idling in the driveway alot though<P>makes sence kindof... well the altnator still should be able to charge it i would think... how could i test this?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tryxalon||03-17-2001||11:24 PM||||When I got the starter out and was poking around, I found that there was 800 ohms resistance along the terminal (bolt like thing to attach the battery cable). Likewise on the small "S" terminal. <P>Since those terminals are copper the resistance should have been zero!! The new starter had Zero resistance along each terminal and between the two. <P>An Ohm meter should tell you if there is excessive resistance measuring across the two terminals (DISCONNECT BATTERY GROUND FIRST!!) even though you'd have to crawl under the car to test it. <P>Probably easier would be to measure the draw of the starter (battery volt drop greater than specifications while starting) should give an indication.  <P>WE know that alternator rectifier diodes can cause a high idle condition on ECM cars because the alternator alt doesn't produce sufficient power to recharge battery so ECM "high Idles" to recharge. <P> I'm just putting together what I found out with that fact and making a logical conclusion that the excessive drain of a degraded connection on the starter circuit could also cause "high idle". <P>My point is: with an ECM controlling the fuel mixture, timing and rest of the system, any significant drain or deficiency in the electrical system could be a cause of an idle around 2000 rpm (charging rate).<P><BR>Hmmm. That means that even possibly a malfunctioning headlight motor (another high amp draw) could cause a high idle if the motor is not disconnected. <P>Could the Auxilary fan on V-6's Also be a high draw?? <BR> <P><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 03-17-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||03-18-2001||03:47 PM||||One of the reasons I rewired my 84 2M4...<BR>I removed the extra wires going to the battery terminal of the starter and ran them to the battery...<BR> That left two wires going to the starter, the Main battery cable, and the start wire...||||reg||1
